00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.390
Not just us-- our
customers, our partners.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:30.110
And what that leads us to is the
question of unstructured data.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.970
So, one of the very sort
of interesting pieces

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:38.140
of statistics or whatever that
a lot of people don't realize

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.320
is that upwards of
90% of enterprise data

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.520
is unstructured.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.460
It's all those emails.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:44.710
It's all those word documents.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:46.780
It's all those text files.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.030
It's all those PDFs
sitting in accounting.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.530
That's all unstructured data.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.480
And the challenge
with unstructured data

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.629
is not only is it
abundant, it's hard.

00:01:55.629 --> 00:01:58.170
You think of structured data,
you think of a database, right?

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.950
The average CTO goes, oh, yeah--

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.250
I have "XYZ" amount of
petabytes in databases,

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.750
and the way you
access that is easy.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.125
You write a bunch
of SQL queries.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:08.440
Or there's a standard
ecosystem that everybody

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.030
reaches for of tools.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.690
But if I gave you a chunk of
unstructured data and say,

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.530
go get me answers--

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:16.890
there's no clear answer.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.490
There is no standard set of
tools that you're going to use.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:20.920
Someone's going
to reach for, you

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.770
know, regular expressions
or some of those things.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.500
But what we're doing
here now is applying ML.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:30.290
And the idea is that
machine learning

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.100
has a very clear
path to making sense

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:38.440
of unstructured,
massive volumes of data.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.920
Part two of the story is that
transformation's coming, right?

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.400
Businesses used to
be run like this.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.350
This is what we're moving to.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:51.680
And you should have started
your journey a while ago,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.990
but if you haven't,
that's still OK.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:55.660
You can still do it now.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.440
And the underlying theme
here is the announcements

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.019
you saw around AutoML--

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:02.560
all these cutting
edge technologies--

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.450
you can apply them
to your business--

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.490
your existing business process--

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:10.310
as you are on this
journey of transformation.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.340
So, the announcement
this morning

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.250
was about Iron Mountain
and our partnership there.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.990
And what are we doing here?

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:21.010
The goal is to advance automated
document understanding.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.530
We're working with
Iron Mountain to--

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.950
with their customers'
permission-- digitize data

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.000
and derive
understanding from it.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.260
So all that dark data that's
sitting stored away from years

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.820
of records-- literally
the US corporate history

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.930
of the Fortune 1000--

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.810
extracting valuable--
today-- dark data

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.680
using machine learning.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.340
But, as all these
presentations go--

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.860
the ML guy comes
on stage and goes

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.360
on about how ML
is going to change

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:50.920
the world in the future--

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.330
we'll get there.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.710
That's true.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.110
But let's talk
about here and now.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:57.760
How can you use
the tools we talked

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.540
about this morning and all day
today and throughout Next--

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.781
AutoML and machine vision to
automate an existing process?

00:04:05.781 --> 00:04:07.030
So this is about here and now.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:11.360
This is something that
you can start using today.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.839
So, as one of the scenarios in
the thousands we could tackle

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:21.430
with how businesses deal with
documents, the one that seems

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:24.940
to impact the most number
of businesses is, hey,

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.260
I buy stuff as a part
of doing business.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.420
I owe people money.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.450
Or I sell stuff and
people owe me money.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.360
And these documents
need to be processed.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.940
We've been doing
that since 1860.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:42.250
This is an actual invoice from
1860, and this is a modern one.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.000
Nothing's really changed.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.290
And the reason it hasn't
changed is the computer science

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:49.960
behind making a
protocol packet to have

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.829
two computers talk about,
"Hey, you owe me X" is easy.

00:04:53.829 --> 00:04:55.120
But there are too many players.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.830
The structure of that packet
cannot be agreed upon.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.100
So what we've done is we've
sort of fallen back to,

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.980
well we have accounts
receiving, and the invoice team

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:07.090
has people that look at it
and approve it and it's a--

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.830
you know, it's a human
intervention process.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.890
So let's change that.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:15.670
So what does it mean to do
document understanding--

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:18.130
at 10,000 feet.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.070
The thing people usually
jump to is, oh this is OCR.

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:22.660
This is not just OCR.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:25.210
OCR is Optical
Character Recognition.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:25.870
Literally.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.600
The "Hello World" of machine
learning for any new learning

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.990
data scientist is a
data set called MNIST.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.210
And this data set is
understanding characters.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.520
It's literally the "Hello
World" of the industry.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:41.140
What we're talking about here is
going from that extracted text

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:46.030
to recreating the structure that
is apparent to a human that's

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.090
reading the document--
recreating that structure

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:50.800
and then putting it
into a system that

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.990
manages structured
data, and it's

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:55.120
part of a business process.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.550
That is truly document
understanding.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:00.130
And at 10,000 feet,
it's these three steps.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:03.350
And where OCR is literally
is just step one.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.580
OK so let's talk about
each of the pieces.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:10.030
Step one-- you extract the text.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.120
So Google has the
Cloud Vision API.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.670
This API solves computer
vision challenges and problems

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.650
that are general in nature.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:24.400
It's not going to identify
my business' widget

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.530
A versus widget
B. But if you want

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.709
to identify a ship
versus a boat,

00:06:29.709 --> 00:06:31.750
it can do that because
this is a general problem.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:32.800
It impacts everything.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.600
This is the
technology that backs

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.570
Google's efforts in search.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:41.590
Part of this offering is
an offering to do OCR.

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.020
So, when you use
Google search, we're

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.480
not just looking at the
metadata around the search.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.630
We're looking into the images
to find irrelevant-- images.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.300
So the technology that
powers that is also what

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:56.660
powers the Vision API to do OCR.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.450
So, OK-- we have step
one-- we give it an image.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:02.580
It gives us the text.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.980
Part two of this is that the
pricing here is a game changer.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.020
I'll let this slide speak
for itself, honestly,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.330
but it's pennies on the dollar.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:15.220
And one of the key friction
points in business--

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:18.450
there's a reason it's hard
to structure businesses

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.960
on a $1 transaction because
the transaction fee completely

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.790
kills any margin you have.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:27.570
So as you're trying to reduce
your costs of transactions--

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.250
reduce the friction
of transactions--

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.540
this becomes very important.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:35.927
So this basically makes the
costs of transactions pennies

00:07:35.927 --> 00:07:36.510
on the dollar.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:40.780
All right.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:41.290
Step two.

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:43.100
So we got the text
out of the document.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:45.674
Now we've got to restructure it.

00:07:45.674 --> 00:07:47.590
We said we're going to
apply machine learning.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:48.880
That's great.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:51.350
Now you're going to
need a data scientist.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.760
And this person will go away
for six months, eight months

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.670
and come back with a model.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:00.940
In that period, you will have
spent lots and lots of money,

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.210
lots and lots of time, lots
and lots of engineering,

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:06.070
and lots and lots of
organizational focus

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:09.580
on solving this problem
where each of these

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:12.670
is a very complex
problem in itself

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:14.990
that engineers specialize in.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:18.500
So we go back to our
first principles.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:20.890
First principles
are that's not OK.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.680
We need to make the technology
available and accessible

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.050
to every developer.

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:29.230
So we have AutoML.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:33.549
The idea behind AutoML is that
all of this becomes one click.

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:36.220
It becomes one API call.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:38.530
You give it the data.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:39.909
You give it the data set.

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:42.010
And you tell it, go train.

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.860
And in a couple of hours
it comes back to you

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.520
with a model that will
have best-in-class accuracy

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:52.450
and then it gives you, again,
a one API call way to say,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.700
go host this in a
scalable way for me

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.380
so that I can just
make requests to it

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.690
and I don't have to
worry about "Well,

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.994
I need the right infrastructure
to do low-latency inference

00:09:01.994 --> 00:09:03.160
on my model--" None of that.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:04.534
You don't have to
worry about it.

00:09:04.534 --> 00:09:07.130
You just tell us, and
we host it for you.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:10.060
So what does AutoML
Vision do in this case?

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:14.080
AutoML Vision is, what's in the
parlance, called a classifier.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:15.250
It builds you a classifier.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.980
You give it classes
of training data

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.830
that say, hey, here's a bunch
of pictures of handbags, shoes,

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.840
and hats, and you train it.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.540
And then at the
end of the day, it

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:28.700
gives you a model that when you
give it a picture of a shoe--

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.700
even one it hasn't seen
before, but it's a shoe.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:32.900
A human would
recognize the shoe.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:34.900
It'll tell you this is a shoe.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:36.480
In concept, pretty
straightforward.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.540
In implementation billions
of dollars of research.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:42.760
So I said it's going
to give you one

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:46.380
of the best in class models.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.660
AutoML produces
more accurate models

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:53.830
with fewer parameters than
practically all the other

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:58.690
what I would call benchmark
vision models today.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.280
So you've got Inception V4.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:04.870
You've got ResNet-152,
Reznext 101, VGG-16,

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.630
and you can see the red
line with AutoML on top

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:10.150
where, depending on how
much time and energy spent

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:14.740
on training it, you get more
and more accuracy with fewer

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.300
and fewer parameters.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:18.800
Now, why does this matter?

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:19.904
This matters because--

00:10:19.904 --> 00:10:21.070
I know what you're thinking.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:24.020
But, well if Google is going to
do it, then it doesn't matter.

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.030
It matters in latency
and compute costs--

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:27.460
that inference time.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:30.190
So once the model
is built, you're

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:32.590
going to make hundreds of
thousands of requests over time

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:33.460
against it.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.640
So if a model has 10%
more parameters in it,

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.790
you're doing 10% more compute
across hundreds and thousands

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:40.970
of requests.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:42.350
So this is important.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:43.830
The other reason
it's important--

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:45.190
so that was sort of this axis--

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:47.560
the other reason it's
important-- this axis--

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:52.060
is that if you get it wrong,
either your business process

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:54.400
has a problem down the line.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.510
Or now you have to employ the
person that you've freed up

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.300
to do a better task than just
scanning an invoice is now

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.610
back in the loop to make sure
that the computer doesn't

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:05.050
screw up.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:06.520
So that's not OK, too.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:09.510
Hence, this is important.

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:12.530
So a little bit of-- sort
of, technical [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:13.370
about AutoML.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:15.860
So, the technology
powering AutoML

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:21.230
is called learn to learn, where
we've got a neural network that

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:24.740
is effectively watching
the model being built

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.650
for you being trained
and tweaking the training

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:29.390
process as it goes.

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:31.470
So effectively-- sounds
like a buzzword--

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:32.070
I get it.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.160
But it's literally a model
building another model,

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:35.180
is what's going on.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.860
And that's called
learning to learn.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.780
And then, along with
that, we're doing

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:40.760
large-scale
hyperparameter tuning

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:44.420
to give you that super
accurate model without you

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.450
having to do any work.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:48.720
If you want to discuss
the internals of this,

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:49.750
I'll be here after, too.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.810
We can geek out.

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:58.800
So, next up-- so we got the
piece for extracting the text.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.810
We got the piece
for well, vaguely--

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.880
we have this thing
that does a classifier.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.040
There are two tools
in our vision box.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.900
How do we put this all together?

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:10.440
I promise that the solution
